1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,630
Oh, definitely. It is a huge pain point.

2
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:09,770
Right. So, you know, if you run complex systems, you know the frustration of watching every single new project just reinvent the wheel.

3
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:19,350
Mm. Like millions of dollars vanish because one ministry builds a digital login portal totally unaware another one did the exact same thing six months ago.

4
00:00:19,430 --> 00:00:20,790
Yeah, it happens all the time.

5
00:00:20,830 --> 00:00:27,670
So to stop that cycle, we are focusing on a critical four to six-week window called Phase Three: Adapt.

6
00:00:28,530 --> 00:00:34,850
And our source today is a briefing on fitting the PAERA framework to a specific program called PROGRESA.

7
00:00:35,190 --> 00:00:41,210
Right. And our mission here is really to understand how architects make these massive whole of government tech decisions.

8
00:00:41,370 --> 00:00:46,050
And, you know, they do it by making deliberate choices about how to source each individual building block.

9
00:00:46,090 --> 00:00:49,490
Wait, so before you even decide what tech to use, you have to set up the rules of the game, right?

10
00:00:49,570 --> 00:00:52,230
Exactly. Before anyone writes a single line of code.

11
00:00:52,290 --> 00:00:54,940
But if PAERA is the baseline framework,

12
00:00:55,710 --> 00:01:00,400
does a program like PROGRESA just, um, copy-paste those rules?

13
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:04,200
I mean, how do local laws even factor into these LoCTI principles?

14
00:01:04,630 --> 00:01:07,790
Well, they don't draft them from scratch, and they definitely do not just copy-paste.

15
00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:11,570
They inherit the baseline principles, but then they localize them.

16
00:01:11,630 --> 00:01:14,650
Localize them, like making them fit the specific legal context.

17
00:01:14,730 --> 00:01:16,910
Right. So take the once only principle. Okay.

18
00:01:16,950 --> 00:01:25,110
PROGRESA points that directly at their National Data Protection Act, and for the reuse before build principle, they align that straight to their existing procurement rules.

19
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:30,120
Ah, I see. But what about, you know, on the ground context specific needs?

20
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:34,070
Yeah. Like I remember seeing something about offline access for rural schools.

21
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:37,170
Yes, exactly. They added that specific requirement.

22
00:01:37,210 --> 00:01:41,480
And here's the key detail. Every single principle gets a written implication attached to it.

23
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:43,910
A written implication, so there is no wiggle room.

24
00:01:43,930 --> 00:01:49,560
Precisely. So later on, when the board has to settle arguments, they just point to that written implication.

25
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:59,310
That makes a lot of sense. So with those localized principles set up, how do the architects actually apply them to the tech decisions for each, uh, building

26
00:01:59,390 --> 00:01:59,650
block?

27
00:02:00,110 --> 00:02:01,829
That is where the sourcing matrix comes in.

28
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:05,899
It gives them four specific choices: build, buy,

29
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:08,030
share, or sandbox.

30
00:02:08,270 --> 00:02:17,150
Okay. A menu of options. But I mean, if a program has the money, why wouldn't they just build their own custom system for everything, make it exactly how they want it?

31
00:02:17,210 --> 00:02:22,050
Because if you build everything from scratch, you end up with isolated systems that just do not communicate.

32
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:24,030
Oh, right. The fragmentation issue.

33
00:02:24,230 --> 00:02:29,850
Exactly. So they are very deliberate. For instance, PROGRESA decided to build the learner register.

34
00:02:29,990 --> 00:02:32,600
Because the register is the authoritative core of the program, right?

35
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:36,730
Right. You cannot let an outside entity own that core data, so you build it.

36
00:02:37,370 --> 00:02:40,130
But for identity, the call is share.

37
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:43,830
Share. Because the government only wants to pay for user logins once.

38
00:02:43,970 --> 00:02:50,330
Exactly. Yeah. They use the National Identity Authorities platform, and they also share the data exchange backbone.

39
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:52,750
Okay, so that covers build and share. What about buy?

40
00:02:53,110 --> 00:02:59,610
Well, they buy exam management because honestly, tracking exams is a solved problem in the market.

41
00:02:59,770 --> 00:03:01,250
No point in reinventing that wheel.

42
00:03:01,510 --> 00:03:07,090
Right. And finally, sandbox. They use that for new untested analytics.

43
00:03:07,150 --> 00:03:10,680
It lets them try the tech in a safe environment before totally committing.

44
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:16,210
So looking at this matrix, it seems like reuse and share is really the default setting here.

45
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:19,250
Oh, absolutely. Building from scratch is the rare exception.

46
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:20,610
You really have to justify it.

47
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:26,590
But wait, if reusing and sharing saves so much money, why do we even need an enterprise architect?

48
00:03:26,630 --> 00:03:30,090
Like, couldn't a strict procurement rule just force everyone to share?

49
00:03:30,310 --> 00:03:33,970
Well, a procurement rule just looks at a single transaction in a total vacuum.

50
00:03:34,170 --> 00:03:35,830
Oh, so it misses the bigger picture.

51
00:03:35,870 --> 00:03:41,990
Exactly. A procurement rule cannot see how the learner register has to integrate with that shared identity platform.

52
00:03:42,030 --> 00:03:44,590
Only an architect sees that whole of government picture.

53
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:48,350
And all this planning leads up to what the briefing calls the third sign off, right?

54
00:03:48,390 --> 00:03:51,590
Yes. That is a massively consequential gate.

55
00:03:52,119 --> 00:03:58,210
The board officially signs off on the localized principle set and that build by share sandbox matrix.

56
00:03:58,310 --> 00:04:02,839
And signing that basically locks in the shape of the entire roadmap that follows.

57
00:04:03,150 --> 00:04:09,490
It does. It forces those deliberate sourcing calls for every building block right there in that four to six-week window.

58
00:04:09,559 --> 00:04:12,790
So you don't have people making isolated decisions a year down the line.

59
00:04:12,990 --> 00:04:15,830
Exactly. It is all about intentionality.

60
00:04:15,850 --> 00:04:19,699
Well, that leaves a pretty stark realization for anyone running these systems.

61
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:22,250
If you skip this deliberate planning phase,

62
00:04:23,110 --> 00:04:28,690
every single project is going to relitigate the build versus reuse debate entirely on its own.

63
00:04:28,790 --> 00:04:30,950
Which is exactly how you bleed money and time.

